1 OCCU filles à marier
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Biographie
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine Forestier a des origines françaises.
2 CONT Catherine Forestier variations: Fortier, Foretier
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine est baptisée le 6 janvier 1640 à La Rochelle (Ste-Marguerite) en Aunis (Charente-Maritime 17300), fille de Jean Forestier et de Julienne Coiffé. Son parrain fut Jean Allard et sa marraine Catherine De Champs (?).[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Catherine est appelée Forestier, Foretier ou Fortier de façon variée dans les actes divers[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT La première mention de Catherine dans la colonie est son mariage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Décès
2 CONT Catherine Forestier, épouse de Jacques Ménard dit Lafontaine, décède le 31 mars 1694 et est inhumée le 1er avril à Boucherville, l'acte lui donnant environ 54 ans.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT -https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Forestier-18
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT MTDNA is H3
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.francogene.com/triangulation/TRI0221.php
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigration: 1657, File a Marier. (a mark above the "a", that I don't know how to do)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine was a Filles à Marier, arriving in Canada in 1657.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Filles à Marier or 'marriageable girls," were 262 brave and adventurous women who were the first single women to set foot in the French colony since its return from the English in 1632.  Sent by relatives and religious organiz
2 CONC ations or enlisting on their own account, these women did benefit from a paid passage and dowry drawn from the King's treasury (as did the Filles du Roi).  They were true pioneers and heroines.  They left their homes in France, ne
2 CONC ver to return again, and found new lives in the New World.
2 CONT --------------------
