1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/h/o/s/Jackie--Hostage/GENE19-0035.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Therese Menard, born 02 October 16763629; died Bef. 17553630; married (1) Jean-Baptiste Deniau 11 February 1697 in Boucherville, Quebec3631; born 02 November 1673 in Montreal, Quebec3632; died 03 November 1708 in Boucherville, Que
2 CONC bec3633; married (2) Jean (Desmarais) Desnoyers 1709 in Longueuil, Chambly, Quebec ("maries a la gaumine- See;"Notes")3634; born Unknown in Origin Unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT  	Marriage Notes for Therese Menard and Jean-Baptiste Deniau:
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is an article on Le Mariage à la Gaumine" it's quite interesting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Busy with the liturgy of the Mass, the priest failed to notice the
2 CONT
2 CONT foursome sitting close to one another in the rear pew. The young lady gazed
2 CONT
2 CONT at the youth beside her in rapt adoration, hardly aware of thecelebrant at
2 CONT
2 CONT the altar. from time to time her companion gave her hand a reassuring
2 CONT
2 CONT squeeze. Unbeknown to the priest and those around them, the young couple
2 CONT
2 CONT were being married in a rite known a " le mariage a la Gaumine".
2 CONT
2 CONT Long before the Catholic settlement in Canada the church had been
2 CONT
2 CONT plagued by the practice of those who, for some reason or another, could not
2 CONT
2 CONT be married within the laws of the Church. The problem of clandestine
2 CONT
2 CONT marriages caused the Council of Trent to declare that a marriage could be
2 CONT
2 CONT valid only when reformed by a priest in the presence of two witnesses. The
2 CONT
2 CONT requirements of the Council were enacted into civil law by the DeBlois
2 CONT
2 CONT ordonnance of 1579 and by the legal decree of Tametsi. The passage of the
2 CONT
2 CONT law and the enforcement of it are two different things, the decrees make
2 CONT
2 CONT little impression on those who wished to sidestep them. They quickly found
2 CONT
2 CONT ways to evade the restrictions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most popular form of evasion was to marry "a la Gaumine",
2 CONT
2 CONT so-called after a certain Mr. Gaumin who had devised the ruse to circumvent
2 CONT
2 CONT both church and legal procedures.
2 CONT
2 CONT Using this method, the engaged couple and their two witnesses would
2 CONT
2 CONT meet at church and, during the Mass, would make their marital commitment to
2 CONT
2 CONT one another in the presence of the two witnesses, but without the knowledge
2 CONT
2 CONT of the priest.
2 CONT
2 CONT The custom of the marriage "a la Gaumine" came to New France with the
2 CONT
2 CONT immigrants and there were those who, in their homeland, resorted to the
2 CONT
2 CONT irregular and illegal ceremony for various reasons. The practice persisted
2 CONT
2 CONT despite the fact that church and legal authorities used every kind of tactic
2 CONT
2 CONT to prevent it. It became such a vogue that in 1717 the Bishop of quebec
2 CONT
2 CONT issued a mandate to anyone contracting a marriage "a la Gaumine" would be
2 CONT
2 CONT subject to excommunication. He cited flouting of church authority,
2 CONT
2 CONT desecration of the church's sacred ceremonies and a side-stepping of
2 CONT
2 CONT parental permission. To add emphasis to his order, he warned that witnesses
2 CONT
2 CONT to such marriages would also face excommunication.
2 CONT
2 CONT In some dictionaries, such as Tanguay etc. and in some historical
2 CONT
2 CONT reportings one will find recorded accounts of such marriages "a la Gaumine".
2 CONT
2 CONT At Boucherville, the marriage of Jean Desnoyers and Therese Menard was
2 CONT
2 CONT celebrated. A few years previously, unknown to their missionary, Rev. de
2 CONT
2 CONT Francheville, who was celebrating Mass, they had married themselves "a la
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaumine". In 1727, while the pastor of Batiscan celebrated the Mass, a
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Portail and Antoinette Langy became husband and wife "a la Gaumine".
2 CONT
2 CONT At St.Jean Port-Joli a young couple who had been refused a
2 CONT
2 CONT dispensation took the matter in their own hands. They erected a makeshift
2 CONT
2 CONT altar at home and while a friend impersinated a priest celebrating Mass,
2 CONT
2 CONT they married one another in a mock ceremony. The repercussions were swift
2 CONT
2 CONT and drastic. The erring couple were excommunicated as were those who acted
2 CONT
2 CONT as witnesses to the affair.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twenty days the couple repented and returned to the embrace of the
2 CONT
2 CONT church and its legal requirements. This episode marked the end of the
2 CONT
2 CONT "marriage a la Gaumine" and the custom became a quaint bit of history; the
2 CONT
2 CONT year was 1774....
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref: "The Genealogist", The American-Canadian Genealigist, Manchester, N.H.
2 CONT
2 CONT done by Edwin J. Allard, a retired N.Y. columnist who now writes on a
2 CONT
2 CONT freelance basis. His wife, Mary, is a retired teacher and researching
2 CONT
2 CONT Desjardins genealogy. 
2 CONT --------------------
