1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Pierre de Roquefeuil-Montpeyroux'''[Note 1] was a French Navy officer. He served during the War of American Independence.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Roquefeuil was born in 1735 to the House of Roquefeuil-Blanquefort. He grew up in Montpeyroux, Aveyron where his family owned the Château du Bousquet.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 19 September 1749, he joined the French Navy as a Garde-Marine. He was promoted to lieutenant on 1 May 1763.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1773, he was made a Knight in the Order of Saint Louis.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 4 April 1777, he was promoted to captain [3] and given command of the 32-gun frigate Oiseau.[2][4] He served Du Chaffault.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1778, named flag captain on the 80-gun ship Saint-Esprit, [2] Roquefeuil participated to the battle of Ushant under Lamotte-Picquet.[6][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1779, he was given command of the frigate Renommée, with which he captured two British ships. He then transferred to the 74-gun Zodiaque.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1781, he commanded the 74-gun Dauphin Royal. He took part in the Battle of the Saintes on 12 April 1782, and was later cleared by the inquiry into the battle.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1783, he earned a membership in the Society of the Cincinnati.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He retired on 8 February 1786 with the rank of brigadier des armées navales,[3] on medical grounds.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre de Roquefeuil-Montpeyroux died in 1789 [citation needed] in Brittany.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT In 1783, Roquefeuil married Léocadie de Lagadec [8] with whom he had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Adolphe, [Note 2]
2 CONT * Alphonse-Louis.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre de Roquefeuil-Montpeyroux is also the nephew of:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques Aymar de Roquefeuil et du Bousquet (1665-1744), [8]
2 CONT Aymar Joseph de Roquefeuil et du Bousquet (1714-1782).
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre_de_Roquefeuil-Montpeyroux Wikipedia]
2 CONT
