1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louise-Julie-Constance_de_Brionne
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise-Julie-Constance de Rohan-Rochefort, Countess of Brionne, born on 5 March 1734, received canoness of the convent of Remiremont on 10 December 1742, married on 3 October 1748 in Paris Louis-Charles de Lorraine, Count of Brion
2 CONC ne, childless widower of Louise-Charlotte de Gramont (1725-1742) and Augustine de Coëtquen (1722-1746). She was a passion perceived as platonic by Louis XV and the mistress of Étienne-François de Choiseul, whom she accompanied in 
2 CONC his exile at the Château de Chanteloup. The only woman to have held the office of Grand Equerry of France, chatelaine of Limours, she emigrated during the French Revolution and died on 20 March 1815 in Pressburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques Wilbault, Louise-Julie Constance de Brionne between the Duc de Choiseul and the Abbé Barthélemy, Getty Center, Los Angeles
2 CONT
2 CONT Plan of Turgot, Place du Carrousel, 1736
2 CONT
2 CONT Castle of Limours
2 CONT
2 CONT Crypt of St. Martin's Cathedral in Bratislava
2 CONT A big family
2 CONT "Great aristocrat with legendary beauty"[1]Louise-Julie-Constance was, after Éléonore-Louise-Constance (1728-?) and Charles Jules Armand de Rohan, Vicomte de Lavedan (1729-1811), the third of the children born of the marriage of C
2 CONC harles de Rohan-Rochefort, Prince of Montauban (1693-1766) with Éléonore Eugénie de Béthisy (1707-1757). A descendant of the Dukes of Brittany, she was received in her ninth year, on 10 December 1742, as a canoness of the Abbey of
2 CONC  Remiremont, a title justifiable by her two hundred years (at least) of ancestry in the nobility[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT She married on 3 October 1748 in the Abbey of Panthemont in Paris (of which her maternal aunt Marie-Catherine de Béthizy de Mézières had been abbess since 1743) Louis-Charles de Lorraine, Count of Brionne (1725-1761)[3], cousin of
2 CONC  Emperor Francis I of the Holy Roman Empire. The marriage was celebrated by Prince Louis-Constantin de Rohan. The couple had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles-Eugène de Lorraine (1751-1825),
2 CONT Joséphine de Lorraine (1753-1797) married in 1768 Victor-Amédée de Savoie, Prince of Carignan (1743-1780)
2 CONT Anne-Charlotte de Lorraine-Brionne (1755–1786), abbess of Remiremont (1775–1786),
2 CONT Joseph-Marie de Lorraine-Vaudémont (1759-1812) married Louise de Montmorency-Logny (1763-1832) in 1778.
2 CONT A strong-willed woman
2 CONT Her husband was the victim of a hunting accident in 1761. Despite the king's promise to the deceased to pass on his duties to his son, the countess had to fight for the monarch to keep his word. A close friend of the Duc de Choise
2 CONC ul, she obtained from the king on the death of her husband, although her son was still a child, the survival of the office of grand equerry of France in his favour. She remains the only woman to have held this office, which she pa
2 CONC ssed on to her son when he reached the age of 20[4]. The court called her "Madame la Grande".
2 CONT
2 CONT The Memoirs of Jean-François Marmontel reconstruct her frequenting Madame du Deffand's salon and also receiving herself in her own salon on the Place du Carrousel: "the new tales that I wrote at the time, and of which these ladies
2 CONC  were the first, were, before or after supper, amusing reading for them. They made an appointment to hear him; and when the little supper was lacking by some event, it was at dinner at Madame de Brionne's that they assembled. I co
2 CONC nfess that never has success flattered me more sensibly than that which my readings had in this little circle, where wit, taste, beauty, all graces were my judges, or rather my applause. There was not a feature, either in my paint
2 CONC ings or in my dialogue, in the least delicate or fine, that was not keenly felt; and the pleasure I caused had the air of rapture."[5].
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 March 1775, the Countess of Brionne bought the Château de Limours from the heirs of Guillaume de Lamoignon de Montevrault, which had belonged successively to Richelieu and Gaston d'Orléans[6]. His decision not to keep the we
2 CONC st wing, which was then very dilapidated, caused the disappearance of the large ballroom with its woodwork and its roof by the architect Philibert Delorme, while offering new lighting to the inner courtyard[7].
2 CONT
2 CONT Disgrace, unpopularity, and exile
2 CONT During the reign of King Louis XVI of France, although she belonged by marriage to the House of Lorraine, from which Queen Marie-Antoinette also came, the Countess of Brionne was not well received at court. The young queen appreci
2 CONC ates above all people of her age and looks gloomy to her elders regardless of their rank.
2 CONT
2 CONT The House of Rohan was also the subject of two resounding scandals: in 1782, the bankruptcy of the Prince of Guémené, whose wife was governess of the "children of France" (the Dauphin and his sisters), then in 1786, the conviction
2 CONC  of the Grand Almoner of France, Cardinal de Rohan, compromised in the affair of the Queen's necklace, highlight the expensive lifestyle of the members of the great families and the corruption of the court, causing the disgrace of
2 CONC  all the members of the House of Rohan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Countess emigrated during the French Revolution. Arrested in a town which, in her Memoirs, Laure Junot d'Abrantès believes to be Chalons-en-Champagne where Jean-Andoche Junot, husband of the narrator, was then with his regimen
2 CONC t: "it was said that Madame de Brionne carried away the diamonds of the crown; she was the mother of the Prince de Lambesc, whose name was abhorred by the people for his affair at the Tuileries; moreover, she was of the house of L
2 CONC orraine, and that was enough to make her suspect: she was therefore arrested; but, thanks to Junot, this step, which could have had a very alarming character for the woman who was the object of it, had no other unfortunate consequ
2 CONC ence than the very fact of her arrest." She was then able to leave, but not before having given Junot a box containing her portrait that he would keep for the rest of his life[8].
2 CONT
2 CONT His youngest daughter, abbess of Remiremont, was in fragile health and died in 1786. The eldest died in 1797. Finally, Prince Joseph, who had emigrated with his brother in 1791 and was an officer in the Austrian army, died in Hung
2 CONC ary in 1812. His only grandchild, the Prince of Carignan, died prematurely in 1800, leaving a son, Charles-Albert. Although the mother of the heir to the throne of Sardinia, the prince's widow, Maria Christina of Saxony, niece of 
2 CONC the King of Saxony, rallied to the empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 20, 1815, a few days after receiving a visit from "his old friend" Talleyrand, who was then at the Congress of Vienna[9], she died in Pressburg, now Bratislava. She rests in the crypt of the cathedral of Saint-Martin. Amo
2 CONC ng her direct descendants (through her daughter Joséphine) was the King of Italy Umberto I, of whom she was the great-great-grandmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judgments
2 CONT "She was beauty itself, and wisdom infused. She had a well-shaped and high waist, with a dignified bearing, an imposing, obliging, and gentle air. She was a Christian Juno, heraldic, and always well powdered, well supported on the
2 CONC  ermines of Brittany and speckled with crosses of Lorraine in profusion. She had a very peculiar way of having her red placed, that is to say, in an absolutely straight line as close as possible to the eyes, the brilliance of whic
2 CONC h was not diminished by this layer of brilliant carmine glazed with silver, while the other three lower and lateral lines were rounded in a curve with perfect grace, at an equal distance from the nose and the ears. and without eve
2 CONC r falling below the level of the mouth, which gave to his whole air of head a great distinction. She had good sense with very good taste, little wit with a charming reserve. She was not always equally satisfied with the good condu
2 CONC ct of her husband (Prince Louis of Lorraine, Grand Equerry of France), as he did not always show himself in good company; so she said to him one day in my presence, with a solemn air of partiality: "Sir, if I meet you again in the
2 CONC  Tuileries, walking there with Messrs. Rivarol and Champcenetz, you may reckon, and I give you my word of honor, that I will bow to them!" - Madame de Créquy[10].
2 CONT "In a hotel in the Place du Carrousel, the company found Madame de Brionne. A princess in the full extent of the word, and with all the outward appearance of pride, she was dignified, imposing, lofty in her bearing, severe in her 
2 CONC manners, and keeping people at a distance. She seemed to count her looks for graces, her words for services, her familiarity for benefits. She had the soul of her face, the warmth, the vivacity she lacked; but the sureness of his 
2 CONC judgment, the finesse of his tact, a rare sense acquired in the practice of political affairs, a facility of speech which was marvellous, the constancy of his friendship, a mixture of stiffness and cold grandeur, won him the respe
2 CONC ct of the world, which approached his drawing-room only with a certain embarrassment. - Edmond and Jules de Goncourt[11]
2 CONT Portraits
2 CONT Louise-Julie-Constance de Brionne
2 CONT Jean-Baptiste Lemoyne (1704-1778), Bust Portrait of Louise-Julie-Constance de Brionne, marble, n°162 of the Salon of 1763, Nationalmuseum, Stockholm[12]. This bust inspired Voltaire to write a few verses[13] :
2 CONT "Brionne, of this admirable bust model,The
2 CONT shaft of virtue as well as of beauty;
2 CONT Friendship consecrates him to posterity,And
2 CONT immortalizes himself with it. »
