1 NAME Louis /Lemaitre/
2 GIVN Louis
2 SURN Lemaitre
2 _MARNM Lemaitre
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 5 JAN 1689
2 PLAC Trois-Rivieres, St Maurice, Quebec, Can.
2 ADDR
1 BAPM
2 DATE 6 JAN 1689
2 PLAC Immaculee Conception de Trois-Rivieres
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 26 JAN 1768
2 PLAC Trois-Rivières,,Mauricie,Quebec,CANADA,
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Louis Maitre (Louis Le Maître) was an artisan from Trois-Rivières, New France, around the 1750s, specializing in the manufacture of canoes and rabaskas of different capacities according to the needs of the coureurs
2 CONC  des bois for the fur trade. He was a member of a family of which several generations made canoes in Pointe-du-Lac, Odanak (Indian reserve) and Wôlinak.
2 CONT -- Canoe design --
2 CONT Louis Franquet, an officer and military engineer, described the workshop in 1752 during his trip to Canada and his stop in Trois-Rivières.
2 CONT "It is in this city that bark canoes are best made; I went to see a building site. One with eight places was being worked on; it was 33 feet long, five wide, two and a half high, and priced at 300 pounds. As they are made, they ar
2 CONC e sent to Montreal; they are intended for the journeys of the Pays-d'en-Haut, as much to carry troops as provisions and merchandise. (...) There is another one (manufacturer, editor's note) who gets involved, but he does not succe
2 CONC ed so well. The first makes such a large quantity that he receives more than 6000 pounds from the king every year; they are worked by women and girls; they are built entirely of birch bark with rounded verandas that are used inste
2 CONC ad of curves; they are of cedar or fir wood, not more than two lines thick, and three inches wide, and the seams, covered with fir gum, are impenetrable to water, but rocks must also be avoided."
2 CONT Roles of the Indians
2 CONT The Indians of the various villages in New France also participated in the work; they mastered the collection and cutting of birch bark pieces; and their wives as well as the young girls were very skilled at sewing the pieces toge
2 CONC ther, with white pine roots.
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT The Canoe in Canadian Cultures published by Bruce W. Hodgins, John Jennings, Doreen Small, 2001.
2 CONT Georges-Hébert Germain (ill. Francis Back), Les Coureurs des bois: La Saga des Indiens blancs, Montréal, Libre Expression, 2003, 158 p. (ISBN 9782764800607).
2 CONT [https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Maitre]
